# Claw lineage

Several claws sit behind the title. They are related. They are not the same talk.

This page is a public-safe map. It does not copy private course design.

## OpenClaw

Peter Steinberger's OpenClaw is the spark: a persistent agent that lives on your computer, with identity files, channels, and a place to inhabit. Joel used it, liked it, and wanted one that was his.

Public notes exist. Do not paste raw interview transcripts here.

## JoelClaw

Joel stopped renting the shape of an agent and built the claw himself. GrimLock came first. Then the owned runtime: Pi as harness, event bus, memory, tools, operating rules.

Published spine: the model is not the agent. The agent is a model inside a harness, operating inside context. Swap the model and the agent can stay yours. Lose the harness and you have a clever stranger in an empty room.

Cascadia said the same thing as a swarm talk: more agents add an orchestration tax. Own the loops. Demand receipts.

## The Claw: Minions of Toil (course)

Same title as this talk. Flagship wizardshit / Vercel Academy course concept. A roguelite colony sim where Zots — minions of toil — work a dungeon generated from a real codebase. The design claim: game mechanics are agent architecture.

The course bible is private. This public repo may use the title, the minion register, and the one-line claim. It may not paste lessons, bookends, or the full concept doc.

## This talk

CYC26, 25 minutes. Swarms as the frame. `ts-cli-template` — and this clone — as the floor those minions stand on.

The dungeon still needs rules. Law in `VISION.md` / `AGENTS.md`. Fence in pins, checks, and hooks. Effect Schema on `data/talk.json`. That is the claw-shaped point without shipping the course or the fleet.

